= Poejo (Pennyroyal)

_Status: classical in Greco-Roman pharmacy under_ βλήχων (blēchōn) / pulegium _— with substantive Hippocratic, Dioscoridean, Plinian, and Galenic treatment; canonical in Tibb under_ fūdhanaj / fawtanaj _— Avicennan_ Hot-and-Dry in the 3rd degree; _foundational in Portuguese-Brazilian folk medicine as_ poejo — _the canonical Lusophone postpartum and respiratory aromatic; not classical in Ayurveda or classical Chinese medicine as a major drug, though related_ Mentha _genus species are widely used in both. Modern Western pharmacology has identified the_ pulegone _abortifacient-and-hepatotoxic concern that constitutes the principal modern safety caveat — and is one of the cleanest cases of a classical-traditional substance with a documented modern toxicity profile requiring careful dose-and-form restriction._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Mentha pulegium_ L., family Lamiaceae (formerly Labiatae). The drug is the *herba* (the dried flowering tops) or the *essential oil* (Pulegii aetheroleum). _Mentha_ from Greek μίνθα (_míntha_), classical name for the genus; _pulegium_ from Latin _pulex_ ("flea") — Pliny gives the etymology that the herb *drives away fleas* (and lice and gnats) when burnt or strewn in dwellings; the *flea-driving* attribution is one of the cleaner Greco-Roman insecticide-aromatic ethnobotanical readings, with the modern pulegone insecticidal pharmacology vindicating it directly.#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _βλήχων_, _μίνθα_); Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _pulegium_, _pulex_). Pliny on the flea-driving attribution: _Naturalis Historia_ XX.54-55. The etymological-and-pharmacological alignment is exact — pulegone has documented insecticide activity, the basis of modern pennyroyal-oil flea-and-tick products in herbal veterinary use (with the major safety caveat — see Modern Pharmacology).]

*English:* _Pennyroyal_, from Anglo-French _puliol real_ ("royal pulegium"), from Old French _puliol_ from Latin _puleium_ (a variant of _pulegium_); the "royal" attribution preserves a medieval Norman-French naming pattern. Common names: _pulegium_, _pudding grass_, _European pennyroyal_ (to distinguish from the American _Hedeoma pulegioides_, a different species in the mint family with similar pharmacology).#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "pennyroyal, n."). The American pennyroyal (_Hedeoma pulegioides_) is a similar-pharmacology distinct species; the two are interchangeable in much modern Western herbal practice but botanically and chemically distinct.]

*Greek:* βλήχων (_blēchōn_) — the Hippocratic and Dioscoridean foundational term; some Hellenic-era authors use γλήχων (_glēchōn_) as a variant. The Greek name is itself of uncertain etymology; possibly pre-Greek substrate.

*Portuguese:* *poejo* (also _poejo-real_ in older Portuguese, preserving the "royal" attribution; _poejo-rasteiro_ for the prostrate form); from Latin _puleium_. The Portuguese name spread to Brazilian Portuguese with the herb's transatlantic transplantation. Note: in some Brazilian regions, the name "poejo" may be applied to other small-leaf mints — including _Cunila microcephala_ (poejo-do-campo / Brazilian native), _Hedeoma multiflorum_, and _Mentha pulegium_ — creating species-identification ambiguity in regional folk-medical contexts. The principal European-imported "poejo" is _M. pulegium_.

*Spanish:* _poleo_ — the cognate term across Iberian Romance; also _poleo menta_; cultivated and traditionally used across Spain and Latin America.

*Italian:* _puleggio_, _menta puleggio_.

*Arabic:* فوذنج (_fūdhanaj_, also _fawtanaj_, _fūthanaj_) — the canonical Tibb name; the Arabic-Persian transmission preserves the substantive Avicennan treatment. Persian: پودنه (_pūdane_), پودنه کوهی (_pūdane-ye kūhī_, "mountain pūdane" — for the wild form). Urdu/Hakeemi: فوتنج (_fawtanaj_); پودینہ (_pudīna_) more commonly refers to garden mint (_M. spicata_, _M. piperita_) in modern Urdu — the pennyroyal-vs-garden-mint nomenclatural distinction matters.

*Sanskrit:* No species-specific canonical entry. The broader _Mentha_ genus is treated under पुदीना (_pudīnā_, Persian-derived Hindi/Sanskrit), but this typically refers to garden mint (_M. spicata_, _M. piperita_), not pennyroyal. The pennyroyal-specific Sanskrit name is essentially absent from the classical canon; modern Hindi uses पोदिना (_podinā_) and various transliterations of the European name.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Not species-specific classical for_ Mentha pulegium. _The broader_ Mentha _genus is canonical in Chinese pharmacy via_ 薄荷 (bòhe, _M. haplocalyx_ Briq.), _the Chinese-native mint species, which is fully canonical in the Bencao tradition with its own substantive treatment — but pennyroyal as a distinct species is post-classical to Chinese pharmacy. Reasoned cross-system placement only._

By reasoned placement on the Chinese-mint axis: cool (涼), pungent (辛), aromatic (芳香); Lung (肺), Liver (肝); reasoned actions 疏風散熱 (disperses wind-heat), 透疹 (promotes rash eruption — by extension to the broader mint family), 清利頭目 (clears the head and brightens the eyes), 解鬱 (resolves constraint), 通經 (regulates menstruation — the canonical pennyroyal-Chinese-mint-genus convergence). The strong-aromatic-pungent character is shared across _Mentha_. Cautions: the pulegone toxicity is the principal modern safety overlay; classical TCM does not distinguish pennyroyal from the other mints at the species level. Reasoned, not citation.#footnote[薄荷 (Mentha haplocalyx) is the canonical Chinese mint species — in _Bencao Gangmu_ and standard Chinese pharmacology; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. _Mentha pulegium_ is a Mediterranean-European species, post-classical to Chinese pharmacy.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Not classical for_ Mentha pulegium _specifically. The broader_ Mentha _genus (_M. spicata, M. piperita, M. arvensis_) entered classical Indian use under the Persian-derived name_ pudīnā _and has been adopted into Ayurvedic compound and dietary practice. Pennyroyal specifically is a post-classical European-Iberian import to South Asia and has no canonical Sanskrit entry. Reasoned cross-system placement only._

By reasoned placement on the broader _pudīnā_ axis: madhura-kaṭu rasa with kaṣāya secondary; laghu-rūkṣa-tīkṣṇa guṇa; *uṣṇa vīrya* (with the pennyroyal-specific *tīkṣṇa-uṣṇa* register stronger than ordinary mint due to the pulegone fraction); kaṭu vipāka. Pacifies Kapha; aggravates Pitta and Vāta in excess. Reasoned karma: _dīpana, pācana_, _vāta-anulomana_, _kāsa-hara_, _śvāsa-hara_ (the canonical respiratory register), _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_, _krimi-ghna_ (anthelminthic), _ārtava-janana_ (emmenagogue — *but with strong abortifacient-toxicity caveat*; see Viruddha M-tier). Strong caution in pregnancy.

The substance does not appear in Caraka, Suśruta, or the major nighaṇṭus by species identification.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(F) Conditional / folk:* Pitta excess; pregnancy (the universal folk-medical caution preserved across Iberian, Roman, and Tibb traditions — the abortifacient register is well known to all the classical sources).
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological — the principal substance-safety category:*
  - *Pulegone hepatotoxicity*: pennyroyal essential oil contains *60-90% pulegone* (a ketone monoterpenoid); pulegone is *metabolized in the liver to menthofuran*, which is *directly hepatotoxic and abortifacient*. *Pennyroyal essential oil ingestion is dangerous* — documented fatal cases of liver failure, coma, and death from ingestion of pennyroyal essential oil, typically in attempts at self-induced abortion in the 19th and 20th centuries. The hepatotoxicity is dose-dependent and well-characterized. *The essential oil of pennyroyal should not be ingested under any circumstances*; topical use should be diluted.
  - *Abortifacient*: documented across folk medicine of Iberia, North Africa, Italy, and Latin America. The mechanism is uterine smooth-muscle stimulation by pulegone, combined with general systemic toxicity. The substance is *absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy*.
  - *The water-infusion (tea) form is substantially safer*: the pulegone content of a normal pennyroyal-tea infusion is *orders of magnitude lower* than the essential-oil ingestion; the water-soluble fraction includes the flavonoids and non-toxic compounds while the lipid-soluble pulegone partitions only modestly into the water. The *traditional folk tea use* — small amounts of dried herb in hot water for postpartum digestive and respiratory support — has a substantially-better safety record than the essential-oil practice. *But still: high consumption of pennyroyal tea over prolonged periods can produce pulegone accumulation*, and the tea preparation is _not_ recommended in pregnancy.
  - *Liver disease*: avoid in any context.
  - *Concurrent CYP3A4-induced drugs*: theoretical hepatic-stress interaction.
  - *Pediatric*: avoid; the immature liver is more susceptible to pulegone toxicity.#footnote[Pulegone hepatotoxicity: extensive case literature on fatal pennyroyal-oil ingestion — Anderson et al., _Annals of Internal Medicine_ (1996) on the canonical fatal cases; pharmacological mechanism review in _Clinical Toxicology_; EFSA opinion on pulegone limits in food. The water-infusion vs. essential-oil safety differential is well documented but should not be over-leveraged: heavy chronic tea consumption remains potentially harmful.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Pennyroyal is fully canonical in Tibb under_ فوذنج (fūdhanaj), _with substantive Avicennan treatment. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* فوذنج (_fūdhanaj_, _fawtanaj_, _fūthanaj_); the related فلانجبين (_filanjibīn_) refers to other related mints. Persian: پودنه کوهی (_pūdane-ye kūhī_, "mountain pūdane"). Urdu/Hakeemi: فوتنج (_fawtanaj_), فودنج (_fūdanaj_).

*Mizāj (temperament):* *Hot and Dry in the 3rd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb*, Book II, _fūdhanaj_). The 3rd-degree Hot-Dry classification converges with the Greek "warming-and-drying" reading (Galen) and is the canonical Tibb placement.

*Af'āl (actions):*
- *Mufattiḥ li-l-suddad* (deobstruent — for hepatic and uterine obstruction).
- *Mudirr li-l-ḥayḍ* (emmenagogue — the canonical Tibb indication; *promotes menstruation*); same mechanism as the abortifacient — flagged as religiously and medically problematic in pregnancy.
- *Mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic).
- *Munaffith* (expectorant) — for chronic phlegmatic cough.
- *Muḥarrik* (stimulant — mild).
- *Mughayyir li-l-balgham al-yābis* (modifies dry phlegm).
- *Muqaṭṭiʿ* (cuts viscous humors).
- *Muqawwī al-miʿda* (gastric tonic, modest).
- *Qātil li-l-dūd* (anthelminthic).
- *Mukhrij li-l-janīn al-mayyit* — *expels dead fetus* — the abortifacient register named with explicit medical justification (used historically for cases of retained dead fetus); the same mechanism makes the substance an abortifacient and dangerous in viable pregnancy.

*Istemālāt (indications):* chronic cold-pattern cough; amenorrhea; retained menstruation; postpartum recovery (in the cold-pattern case); phlegmatic dyspepsia; intestinal worms; topical for joint pain and ear infection.

*Mawāne' (contraindications):* *pregnancy — absolute*; bilious/hot-pattern conditions; hemorrhoids with bleeding; severe peptic ulcer; liver disease.

*Muṣliḥ:* sugar; rose-water; almond oil for the irritating tendency.

*Badal:* garden mint (*naʿnaʿ*); rue (*sadhāb*) for the emmenagogue function (with similar safety caveats).

*Tibb formulas:* *Joshānda-yi Fūdhanaj* — pennyroyal decoction for cough and menstrual stagnation; *Sufūf-i Fūdhanaj* — powder; *Sharbat-i Fūdhanaj* — syrup; *Marham-i Fūdhanaj* — topical ointment for joint and ear pain. The Persian-Iranian folk use of *pūdane-ye kūhī* in regional cooking and tea is contemporary continuation.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _fūdhanaj_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; al-Rāzī, *al-Ḥāwī*; Anṭākī, *Tadhkirah*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III. The pregnancy contraindication and the emmenagogue indication are both standard across all the classical sources.#footnote[Tibb fūdhanaj: Avicennan classical reading with the abortifacient caution universally noted; the cross-system convergence with Greco-Roman, Lusophone, and modern pharmacology on the pregnancy contraindication is essentially complete.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas use _M. pulegium_ specifically; for the broader Chinese mint (_M. haplocalyx_, 薄荷), see the canonical Chinese pharmacy literature. The Mediterranean-pennyroyal-as-mint adaptation is post-classical.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas use _M. pulegium_ specifically. The broader pudīnā (_M. spicata_, _M. arvensis_) is used in modern Indian-Unani cooking and as a digestive carminative; pennyroyal-specific use in modern Indian-Hakeemi practice follows the Tibb framework above.

=== Western / Greco-Roman, Mediterranean, and Iberian-Brazilian folk medicine

This is the substantive tradition for pennyroyal-as-poejo.

- *Greco-Roman:* Hippocratic Corpus uses pennyroyal extensively for gynaecological and respiratory complaints. *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ III.31, _blēchōn_) gives substantive treatment: warming, drying, expectorant, emmenagogue, anthelminthic, antitoxin (snakebite), and *abortifacient* (the classical authors document this explicitly). *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XX.54-55) treats pennyroyal at length, including the famous *flea-driving* attribution. *Galen* classes as warming and drying in the 3rd degree (the source of the Arabic 3rd-degree mizāj).
- *Roman culinary use*: pennyroyal in the *Conditum* (spiced wine), in *moretum* (the garlic-and-herb paste), and as a flea-driving strewing herb in households (the substance literally gave its name to the flea-driving function).
- *Medieval European*: continuous folk use; the Anglo-Saxon *Bald's Leechbook* records pennyroyal preparations for respiratory and digestive disorders.
- *Iberian-Lusophone tradition (the substantive Portuguese-Brazilian register):*
  - *Chá de poejo* — the canonical Portuguese-Brazilian pennyroyal tea; for cold-pattern cough, postpartum recovery (the famous *resguardo* / postpartum-rest period), digestive complaints, mild headache, and respiratory infection. Universal in traditional Portuguese-Brazilian home medicine.
  - *Açorda à alentejana* — Alentejan bread-soup (bread + olive oil + garlic + poejo + water + poached egg + sometimes salted cod); the *foundational rural Alentejan dish* with poejo as its defining aromatic. The combination of poejo with olive oil and bread is climate-and-constitutionally appropriate for the warm-dry Alentejan summer.
  - *Caldo de poejo* — pennyroyal broth.
  - *Postpartum use in Portuguese-Brazilian tradition*: small cups of poejo tea daily during the *resguardo* (the postpartum 30-day rest period) — to support uterine recovery, milk production, and digestive function. The classical-folk *uterine-recovery* register; the abortifacient-emmenagogue mechanism is constitutionally the right action *after* delivery (helping the uterus contract and expel residuals) while being the wrong action *before* delivery.
- *Brazilian-Northeastern* and *Caribbean* folk tradition: pennyroyal as an aromatic-digestive in regional cooking and as a folk respiratory remedy.
- *Modern Western (19th-21st c.):* pennyroyal historically used in 19th-c. American folk medicine and was responsible for the famous *pennyroyal-oil-abortion fatalities* that led to its substantial removal from US herbal-pharmacy mainstream by the late 20th c.; the substance survives in specialty herbal practice with strict pregnancy contraindication.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Hippocrates _passim_; Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ III.31 (Beck translation); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XX.54-55 (Loeb / Rackham); Galen, _De Simplicium Medicamentorum Facultatibus_. Roman culinary: Apicius _De Re Coquinaria_. Iberian-Brazilian tradition: standard Portuguese / Brazilian regional cuisine and folk-medical literature (e.g. Câmara Cascudo _História da Alimentação no Brasil_). Modern fatal cases: Anderson et al., _Annals of Internal Medicine_ (1996) and subsequent pharmacovigilance literature.]

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic) medicine

See above. *Joshānda-yi Fūdhanaj*, *Sufūf-i Fūdhanaj*, *Sharbat-i Fūdhanaj*, *Marham-i Fūdhanaj* are the principal preparations.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Poejo / pennyroyal is principally a *warm-dry Mediterranean* substance — its native range covers the Mediterranean basin, the Atlantic Iberian fringe, and the Middle East — and post-Columbian South American introduction reaches *warm-dry coastal Brazilian-Northeastern* and *temperate-subtropical Atlantic-Brazilian* regions. See _climates.typ_ for the framework.

*Warm-dry Mediterranean (Iberia, Greco-Roman, North African — the canonical cultivation and traditional-use region).* The Mediterranean climate carries hot dry summers and cool wet winters; the pennyroyal plant produces its volatile-pulegone-rich foliage as a summer drought-and-insect-deterrent strategy. In Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ terms the climate carries _grīṣma_ Pitta load with mild _hemanta_-_śiśira_ Vāta; in 中醫 terms 暑燥 (summer-heat-dryness) on the Lung-Heart and mild winter 寒 on the Kidney; in Tibb terms summer ṣafrā' (yellow-bile) and winter cold-dry sawdā'. *The traditional response and the poejo register*: the *winter and cool-season* use of pennyroyal as a warming-aromatic respiratory drug fits the Tibb Hot-Dry character at the right climatic-constitutional moment; the *summer use* is moderate and typically paired with cooling muṣliḥ (olive oil, sugar, rose-water, bread). The *Alentejan açorda* combines bread, olive oil, water, and pennyroyal in a constitutional response to the hot-dry Alentejan climate — the bread provides the cooling-grounding substrate; the olive oil the snigdha-moistening; the pennyroyal the digestive-aromatic-warming counterpoint; the water and salt the climatic-rehydration register. The full preparation is a classical-folk climate-and-constitutional engineering.

*Atlantic-Brazilian subtropical-humid (the principal Brazilian use-region).* Transplanted from Portugal in the colonial period, poejo is widely grown in Atlantic-Brazilian gardens and is used in the Portuguese-Brazilian *postpartum and respiratory-cold tradition*. The hot-humid subtropical climate carries Pitta-Kapha load and 濕熱 (damp-heat) registers; pennyroyal's hot-dry character is *not* constitutionally aligned with the climate as a daily / chronic intake, and traditional Brazilian use accordingly restricts poejo to *acute / episodic* applications (specific respiratory illness, postpartum recovery, intermittent digestive use) rather than the daily-tea register typical of warmer-and-drier climates.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Pennyroyal exported globally as herbal tea reaches every climate; the *constitutional logic* points to cool-season / acute-illness use rather than daily consumption. *The pregnancy contraindication transcends all climate questions* — the abortifacient mechanism is climate-independent and is the principal modern safety overlay. The 19th-20th-c. fatal cases of pennyroyal-oil ingestion in the US and UK occurred largely in temperate-Atlantic climates where pennyroyal was *not* part of the constitutional dietary register — a classical case of _deśa-viruddha_ combining with mistaken-purpose use (essential oil instead of tea, abortion instead of respiratory) to produce maximum harm.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Volatile oil (1-3% of dried herb)*: dominantly *pulegone (60-90% of the oil)*; *menthone* (10-30%); *isomenthone*; *pulegone derivatives* including the metabolite *menthofuran* (formed in vivo by hepatic CYP-mediated metabolism — the toxic species).
- *Flavonoids*: diosmin, hesperidin, eriocitrin, luteolin glycosides (the water-soluble fraction, with mild antioxidant pharmacology).
- *Triterpenes*: ursolic acid, oleanolic acid.
- *Tannins*: modest.#footnote[Phytochemistry: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); analytical reviews in _Phytochemistry_ and _Food Chemistry_ on _Mentha pulegium_ volatile oil composition. The pulegone-menthofuran metabolic pathway is the principal toxicological concern.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Pulegone metabolism and hepatotoxicity*: pulegone is hepatically metabolized by *CYP2E1 and CYP1A2* to *menthofuran*, which forms reactive intermediates that *deplete glutathione* and produce centrilobular hepatic necrosis. The mechanism is similar to the well-characterized acetaminophen / paracetamol hepatotoxicity at high doses. *Pulegone is also directly abortifacient* via uterine smooth-muscle stimulation, and the pregnancy contraindication is mechanistically clear.
- *Carminative / antispasmodic*: the broader mint-family register; clinically meaningful for digestive complaints at safe doses.
- *Expectorant*: the volatile-oil register; classical respiratory use.
- *Antimicrobial*: pulegone and menthone have broad in vitro antibacterial and antifungal activity.
- *Insecticidal*: pulegone is *substantively insecticidal*; modern interest in pennyroyal as a botanical pesticide for flea-and-tick control (with caveats about pet safety — cats are particularly susceptible to pulegone toxicity).
- *Emmenagogue / abortifacient* — the principal safety overlay (above).
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Tibb 3rd-degree Hot-Dry / Greco-Roman warming-and-drying / modern pulegone insecticide-and-emmenagogue-and-hepatotoxic profile converges across all four canonical traditions. The classical pregnancy contraindication is universal (Hippocrates, Dioscorides, Pliny, Galen, Avicenna, modern toxicology).

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Dried herb in tea*: ~1-2 g per cup, 1-2 cups daily for acute use; *not for daily long-term consumption*; *not for pregnancy under any circumstance*.
- *Tincture*: ~1-3 mL standardized (1:5 in 40% ethanol); short-term use.
- *Essential oil — NOT FOR INTERNAL USE under any circumstances*. The essential-oil ingestion is responsible for documented fatalities; *avoid absolutely*.
- *Topical essential oil*: diluted (~1-3% in carrier oil) for short-term application; flea-and-tick repellent register; not for prolonged or large-area use.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid)*:
  - *Pregnancy — absolute avoidance*: the abortifacient mechanism is mechanistically clear and historically documented in fatal-case literature.
  - *Liver disease*: avoid; CYP2E1-induced hepatic stress is dangerous in any context.
  - *Concurrent CYP2E1-inducing drugs* (chronic alcohol, isoniazid): increased pulegone-to-menthofuran metabolism and toxicity risk.
  - *Pediatric*: avoid concentrated forms.
  - *Cats* (relevant for topical / household use): cats are particularly susceptible to pulegone toxicity due to limited hepatic glucuronidation; *pennyroyal essential oil in pet flea products is hazardous to cats*.
  - *Severe Pitta-pattern or bilious patients* (classical caveat preserved).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

